              "text": "The full quote is: \n\n> **\"Businesses owned by responsible and organized merchants will eventually surpass those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\n### Origin and Context\nThis quote is attributed to the 14th-century Arab Muslim philosopher, historian, and sociologist **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406). It is found in his legendary 1377 work, the ***Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena*), which served as the introduction to his universal history of the world. \n\nCenturies before Adam Smith published *The Wealth of Nations*, Ibn Khaldun used this text to lay the early foundations for sociology, demography, and free-market economics.\n\n### The Core Meaning\nIn this quote, Ibn Khaldun is critiquing **state-run commerce** and state monopolies, arguing that private enterprise is far more efficient and sustainable than government-run business. \n\nHis argument in the *Muqaddimah* hinges on several key economic principles:\n\n1. **The Danger of Coercion in the Market:** Ibn Khaldun observed that when rulers or governments engage in trade or agriculture (what he termed \"the commercial activity of the ruler\" or *tij\u0101rat al-sul\u1e6d\u0101n*), they inevitably distort the market. Because rulers possess political and military power, they can force farmers and merchants to sell goods to them at artificially low prices, and then force the public to buy those goods at artificially high prices. \n2. **The Destruction of Incentives:** When a ruler forces private merchants out of the market or undercuts them through unfair, state-backed competition, the incentive for private citizens to produce, trade, and innovate is destroyed. When incentives vanish, economic growth halts, tax revenues dry up, and the state ultimately weakens.\n3. **The Efficiency of Private Merchants:** In contrast, \"responsible and organized merchants\" must survive solely on their business acumen, financial discipline, risk management, and understanding of supply and demand. Because private merchants do not have the luxury of using state coercion to bail themselves out, they are forced to run highly optimized, organized, and customer-centric operations. Over time, these private enterprises prove far more resilient and prosperous than state-sponsored monopolies.",
